            <ArticleTitle>Effect of opiate receptor blockade on pain sensitivity in the rat.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>157-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Blockade of opiate receptors by naloxone (2 mg/kg) was found to produce a significant increase in pain sensitivity as measured by the tail-flick test. This finding supports the view that endogenous opiate systems may play a role in the modulation of pain sensitivity. Naloxone, however, was found to have no effect on pain responsiveness as measured by tail-pinch. These findings, together with additional reports, suggest that endogenous opiate systems may exert differential actions on different sensory modalities.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
